    <name>
      <text>Evaluating the Limit of a Multivariable Function</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Determine if the limit of the function below exists or not. Fill in the missing computational steps.</p>
<p>\( \displaystyle \lim_{(x,y) \to ({@var_x@},{@var_y@})} {@question_func@}\)</p>
<br>
<p>Recall, \(  \displaystyle \lim_{(x,y) \to (x,y)} \frac{ f(x,y)}{g(x,y)} = \frac{\lim_{(x,y) \to (x,y)}  f(x,y)}{\lim_{(x,y) \to (x,y)}  g(x,y)} \) where \( \lim_{(x,y) \to (x,y)}  g(x,y) \neq 0\) </p>
<br>
<p>First, let’s check if the limit of the function in the denominator will give us a zero. </p>
<p>\( \displaystyle \lim_{(x,y) \to ({@var_x@},{@var_y@})} ({@func_denom@}) =\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]][[feedback:prt1]]</p>
<br>
<p>From here, we can proceed as follows;</p>
<p>\( \displaystyle \lim_{(x,y) \to ({@var_x@},{@var_y@})} {@question_func@} = \frac{\lim_{(x,y) \to  ({@var_x@},{@var_y@})}  ({@func_num@}))}{\lim_{(x,y) \to ({@var_x@},{@var_y@})}  ({@func_denom@})}=\)  [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p>
<br>
<p>Therefore, we can say that the limit of this function [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p>To determine if the limit of the given function \( \displaystyle \lim_{(x, y) \to ({@var_x@},{@var_y@})} {@question_func@} \) exists </p>
<p> Recall, \(  \displaystyle \lim_{(x,y) \to (x,y)} \frac{ f(x,y)}{g(x,y)} = \frac{\lim_{(x,y) \to (x,y)}  f(x,y)}{\lim_{(x,y) \to (x,y)}  g(x,y)} \) where \(  \displaystyle  \lim_{(x,y) \to (x,y)}  g(x,y) \neq 0\) </p>
<p>First, we need to check the limit of the denominator:</p>

<p>\( \displaystyle  \lim_{(x, y) \to ({@var_x@},{@var_y@})} ({@func_denom@}) \)</p>

<p>Now, evaluate this limit:</p>

<p>\( \displaystyle \lim_{(x, y) \to ({@var_x@},{@var_y@})} ({@func_denom@}) = {@cc@}({@var_x@})({@var_y@}^2)+{@dd@}({@var_y@}) =  {@cc*var_x*var_y^2@}+{@dd*var_y@} = {@ta1@} \)</p>

<p>Since the limit of the denominator is not zero \(({@ta1@}  \neq 0\)), we can proceed with finding the limit of the entire function using the provided formula:</p>

<p>\( \displaystyle \lim_{(x,y) \to ({@var_x@},{@var_y@})} {@question_func@} = \frac{\lim_{(x,y) \to  ({@var_x@},{@var_y@})}  ({@func_num@})}{\lim_{(x,y) \to ({@var_x@},{@var_y@})}  ({@func_denom@})}\)</p>

<p>Now, let's calculate these limits separately:</p>

<p>For the numerator:</p>

<p>\( \displaystyle \lim_{(x, y) \to ({@var_x@},{@var_y@})} ({@func_num@}) = {@aa@}({@var_x@}^2)({@var_y@})+{@bb@}({@var_x@})({@var_y@}) = {@aa*(var_x^2)*var_y@}+{@bb*var_x*var_y@} = {@aa*(var_x^2)*var_y+bb*var_x*var_y@} \)</p>

<p>Now, we can find the limit of the entire function:</p>

<p>\(\displaystyle \frac{\lim_{(x,y) \to  ({@var_x@},{@var_y@})}  ({@func_num@})}{\lim_{(x,y) \to ({@var_x@},{@var_y@})}  ({@func_denom@})}  = \frac{{@aa*(var_x^2)*var_y+bb*var_x*var_y@}}{{@ta1@}} = {@ta2@} \)</p>

<p>So, the limit of the given function as \( \displaystyle \lim_{(x, y) \to ({@var_x@},{@var_y@})} {@question_func@} \) {@ta3a@} and is equal to \( \displaystyle {@ta2@}\).</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
